From: Magnus Reimann <magnus.reimann@mailbox.org>
Date: Thu, 26 Nov 2026 22:53:09 +0100
Subject: [PATCH gnumach 00/22] SMP fixes, lock debugging, and SMP tests

Hello,

This series comes out of running Debian GNU/Hurd amd64 with all
processors in the default processor set, and stress testing it on 2
and 4 vCPUs (QEMU), and of running Chromium on it.  It contains:

- fixes for SMP bugs found on the way (01-05): per-cpu reads cached by
  the compiler across thread_sleep, fs/gs bases accepted from user space
  that make wrmsr fault or defeat the swapgs check, panic_lock
  reinitialized on every panic, the com driver touching the tty without
  its lock from the interrupt and timer handlers (swapped characters on
  the serial console), and threads held for an empty processor set
  never being released;
- simple lock debugging that works on SMP (06-08);
- stopping the other processors on panic and shutdown (09);
- a smp_pset=default kernel option that puts the APs into the default
  processor set at boot, so that ordinary tasks use them (12);
- the uptime clock going backwards by up to a tick on SMP: a reader
  could combine the new tick time with the old counter value (18).
  V8 aborts on it ("negative elapsed time");
- struct vm_page flags protected by the page queues lock and by the
  object lock sharing one memory location, so that updating one kind
  could undo a concurrent update of the other (21).  Caught with gdb as
  a vm_page_seg_add_active_page assertion while running Chromium;
- double paging in vm_page_evict_one reusing a page already handed to
  an external pager, which on SMP may use or release it concurrently
  (22).  Found by inspection, not observed;
- SMP tests (10, 11, 13-17, 19, 20): a stress test, and focused tests
  for TLB shootdowns, concurrent zero-fill faults, brk-style shrinking
  and growing while other processors have the pages in their TLB,
  glibc-style low-level locks with gsync, a malloc-like heap under one
  such lock, preemption of busy threads on every processor, and a
  monotonic uptime clock.  test-tlb was checked to catch a kernel with
  the shootdown IPI removed (millions of stale reads on 2 processors),
  test-clock to catch the kernel without patch 18 (3 runs out of 3).

Testing: every user test (20, the new ones included; test-multiboot is
skipped as before) passes on an SMP kernel (--enable-ncpus=8
--enable-apic, -smp 2) and on a uniprocessor kernel; both i386
configurations build with no new warnings.  With this series and the
userland fixes sent separately to bug-hurd (hurd, netdde) and
libc-alpha (glibc), Debian GNU/Hurd amd64 boots with smp_pset=default
on 2 vCPUs and passes a parallel build/sort/tar/fork stress test 6
times out of 6; Chromium has run on it for 100 minutes in total
without a kernel panic since patch 21 (before, it panicked within an
hour in 2 runs out of 5).

Patches 06-09 are larger; I am happy to split or rework them.

This work was done together with Claude (an AI model by Anthropic),
hence the Co-Authored-By trailers.

Magnus Reimann (22):
  x86: do not let the compiler cache per-cpu reads
  x86_64: reject kernel and non-canonical fs/gs bases
  debug: do not reinitialize panic_lock on every panic
  com: lock the tty in the interrupt and timer handlers
  machine: release threads held for an empty processor set
  lock: make simple_lock_taken meaningful on SMP
  lock: only check for held simple locks when the lock can sleep
  lock: track simple lock holders on SMP with MACH_LDEBUG
  smp: stop the other processors on panic and shutdown
  tests: add an SMP stress test
  tests: accept KERN_INVALID_ARGUMENT from a terminating thread
  smp: add a smp_pset=default kernel option
  tests: make test-smp exercise TLB shootdowns and task creation
  tests: add test-tlb, check that TLB shootdowns reach all processors
  tests: add test-fault and test-brk for concurrent faults and brk-style
    remapping
  tests: add test-lll, check mutual exclusion of glibc-style low-level
    locks
  tests: add test-heap, mimic a single malloc arena growing and
    shrinking
  clock: do not let readers combine a new tick time with the old counter
  tests: add test-preempt, check that busy threads are preempted
    everywhere
  tests: add test-clock, check that the uptime clock never goes
    backwards
  vm: do not let page flags under different locks share a memory
    location
  vm: do not reuse a page handed to an external pager when double paging

